    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 264,
        "text": "Okay, everyone, thanks for coming. This is the weekly review panel for the city's new school-placement algorithm. We have three items: the data we used, the fairness checks, and the appeal process. Let's start with the data. Tomas, you looked at the numbers.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "857e328d1799b5bd9ef48c96165838f0316876fe2698a3b9f6ef3d4e45c7ea91",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "tomas",
        "speaker": "Tomas",
        "sourceStart": 273,
        "sourceEnd": 585,
        "text": "Yes. The algorithm placed 12,400 students this year. That's about 8 percent more than last year, when it placed 11,500. The main reason is that the city grew. But here's the thing: the share of students who got their first-choice school fell from 71 percent to 64 percent. So more students, but fewer happy ones.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "7815d9b256630b001a31ba1356e15996737089e85b1a00fd12be062a392f9a89",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 593,
        "sourceEnd": 616,
        "text": "That's a big drop. Why?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "5cd99c7b6be06ef21765d277d760bcd9d939707e5fcb931d031b3762a4dd9d83",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "tomas",
        "speaker": "Tomas",
        "sourceStart": 625,
        "sourceEnd": 980,
        "text": "Two reasons. First, more students applied to the same popular schools. Second, we changed the tie-breaker. Last year, distance from home was the only tie-breaker. This year, we added a sibling rule: if an older brother or sister already attends the school, the younger child gets priority. That helped some families but made competition harder for others.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "c4a669c9512ab7d4309eeb9fa2cf32186bed716288eab753c0725d4982d6ea22",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 989,
        "sourceEnd": 1236,
        "text": "Can I jump in? I think the sibling rule is fair in principle, but it has an unintended effect. Families with more children get an advantage. A family with one child has no chance to use it. So the rule favors larger families. Is that what we want?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "a7400b9cb53d401920e6e0481ef038a56310bd69777bc0b56d18ed63ac2c715c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "tomas",
        "speaker": "Tomas",
        "sourceStart": 1245,
        "sourceEnd": 1390,
        "text": "That's a good point. But without the sibling rule, we had complaints from parents who wanted their children at the same school. It's a trade-off.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "bc54f0af5fa1989e04caa937f505b388f5891cbc340f6104d6497be91a0f69df",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1398,
        "sourceEnd": 1485,
        "text": "Let's park that for a moment and look at the fairness checks. Priya, you ran the audit.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "30ff90862c7b9c87fd8a8f7361f0b976b326d420297c7ef71645913f89c0f2ef",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 1494,
        "sourceEnd": 1830,
        "text": "Right. We checked outcomes by income group. For low-income students, the first-choice rate was 58 percent. For middle-income students, it was 66 percent. For high-income students, it was 72 percent. So there is a gap of 14 percentage points between the lowest and highest groups. Last year, the gap was 9 points. So the gap has widened.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "588cdc23c5834156556eeab245dfe5099fbd17836a2804ffaea9e4527b4dd9b3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1838,
        "sourceEnd": 1872,
        "text": "That's concerning. Do we know why?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "bc2dd5e2efeb2d4be28809ba7ca0cc8253429f2f6611b62613bbe821a130d1ae",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 1881,
        "sourceEnd": 2206,
        "text": "Partly because high-income families tend to live closer to popular schools, and distance still matters. Also, they have more resources to navigate the system. But I want to be careful: the algorithm itself doesn't use income. It uses distance, sibling status, and a random number. The gap comes from the inputs, not the code.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "0b2e875f722356c61dce42b6a4d56d51b6f865b59c443de0dca9bb05ce0ac1ab",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "tomas",
        "speaker": "Tomas",
        "sourceStart": 2215,
        "sourceEnd": 2293,
        "text": "That's an important distinction. The algorithm is neutral, but the data isn't.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "d871b578ea7006491fcc5a441d75535fe8577b59249c7a248b2b87e5a94b950c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2301,
        "sourceEnd": 2363,
        "text": "Okay. Now the appeal process. How many appeals did we receive?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "832da50b7794ab3bf7c64c8c6d0354a9a9d68e30427c571160c93495f52333fe",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 2372,
        "sourceEnd": 2647,
        "text": "We received 430 appeals. Of those, 290 were about the sibling rule. The rest were about distance calculations. We resolved 380 appeals, and 50 are still open. The average resolution time was 11 days, down from 18 days last year. So we are faster, but we still have a backlog.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "cad5426182b2de4a15c94bc1beefaf6819e5f1a7ebf619f49e1bfad2c0a3929a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2655,
        "sourceEnd": 2739,
        "text": "And what about the outcomes of the appeals? Did any students get a different school?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "4083400873ef05d0ae3c69ba4a573acfef576d05b4ff0e69d7daf6c0c37c66b1",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 2748,
        "sourceEnd": 2969,
        "text": "Yes. In 120 cases, we changed the placement. Most of those were due to data errors, like a wrong address. Only 15 were due to the sibling rule being applied incorrectly. So the system is mostly working, but errors happen.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "58af431bd0110b67e832ef754213bdf14286558e767b0be0520f047851abd9ed",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "tomas",
        "speaker": "Tomas",
        "sourceStart": 2978,
        "sourceEnd": 3284,
        "text": "I'd like to add one more thing. We should compare this year's algorithm with the old paper-based system. The old system took about six weeks to produce results. The new one takes four days. That's a huge improvement in speed. But speed isn't everything. We need to make sure the faster system is also fair.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "14b3d07a6e371dd02c4ebf1ac3f07f9e41c379853eda8c36ed4d94aae3d88ce2",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3292,
        "sourceEnd": 3473,
        "text": "Agreed. So where does that leave us? We have more students, a lower first-choice rate, a wider income gap, and a faster but still imperfect appeal process. What should we recommend?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "a38c05fc3e3da0d95c5e334f897a0a5c99ba58f57a866f3142275fd5af56df82",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 3482,
        "sourceEnd": 3602,
        "text": "I suggest we keep the sibling rule but add a review after one year. And we should improve address data to reduce errors.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "a08a207f7d4ef806554f529e0007f49772bd0e1a66e2c0b12ec1a5e6439f565a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "tomas",
        "speaker": "Tomas",
        "sourceStart": 3611,
        "sourceEnd": 3755,
        "text": "I agree with the review. But I'd also like to see a simulation of what would happen if we removed the sibling rule. That would give us evidence.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "65470478def6b17f8c6953d0a4fc4e514da1cb9a726d6dae7d57b3cea29df6b2",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3763,
        "sourceEnd": 3974,
        "text": "Good. Let's summarize: we will recommend a one-year review of the sibling rule, a data-quality project for addresses, and a simulation of alternative rules. Any objections? No? Then we're done. Thanks, everyone.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "5f07a4c94e7be0dfa932555c79a690e5fe247d081cf33d73f6b4dfd544262249",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
